  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Bamun\"\nsubtitle: \"Cameroon\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Bamun are a people of Cameroon’s highlands. |\n| Where | Cameroon’s highlands |\n| How many | Approximately 600,000–700,000 Bamum speakers in 2025 |\n| Language | Bamum, also called Shü Pamom, Shümom, Shupamem, Bamun, or Bamoun |\n| Religion | Not documented in the profile |\n| Known for | King Njoya’s original script · Brass figures · Smoking-pipes · Masks · Woven fibre bags |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Bamun live in Cameroon’s highlands. Their language is Bamum, an Eastern Grassfields language with about 600,000–700,000 speakers in 2025. King Njoya and his palace circle developed an original Bamum script around 1895. Museum records document brass figures, smoking-pipes, masks, a staff finial, a palm-wine bottle, a woven bag, and an arm-dagger with sheath.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe documented material culture includes brass objects, smoking-pipes, masks, figures, woven fibre goods, leather sheaths, and a palm-wine bottle.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nThe profile does not name Bamun textile traditions, but it records a woven fibre bag.\n\n- **Woven bag** — The bag is woven from raffia or sisal, with striped fringe, green X-shaped decoration, and a braided brown-fibre handle with tassels.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nMuseum records show brass objects, smoking-pipes, a knife, a woven bag, and a palm-wine bottle.\n\n- **Smoking-pipe** — The catalogues record cast brass smoking-pipes and pipe-bowls, including bowls shaped like an elephant head or two human heads with crocodiles.\n- **Snake staff finial** — A brass staff finial shows six intertwined snake representations.\n- **Tree-trunk bowl** — A carved pottery bowl imitates a tree trunk ending in a face with bulging cheeks.\n- **Palm-wine bottle** — A museum catalogue records a palm-wine bottle with bead mosaic.\n- **Small knife** — The knife has a steel blade, a wooden handle, and a leather sheath.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\n- **Hip mask** — The records include a cast brass hip mask shaped as a face with a beard of mudfish.\n- **Pendant-mask** — The records mention a pendant-mask.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nBamum is used in song, and museum records show a xylophone figure and decorated smoking-pipes.\n\n- **Bamum song** (*Bamum*) — Cameroonian musician Claude Ndam was a native Bamum speaker and sang in the language.\n- **Xylophone** — A brass figure shows a man with a xylophone.\n- **Elephant-head pipe** — One smoking-pipe has a bowl shaped like an elephant head with its trunk raised.\n- **Two-heads pipe** — Another pipe-bowl represents two human heads flanked by two crocodiles.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nA wooden dance mask is recorded.\n\n- **Wooden dance mask** — A British Museum register records a wooden dance mask.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe sources do not describe Bamun foods or cooking, but they record a palm-wine bottle.\n\n- **Palm wine bottle** — A bead-mosaic bottle is recorded.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nThe sources do not document Bamun oral genres or literary works, but they describe the Bamum script.\n\n- **Bamum script** (*Bamum*) — King Njoya and his palace circle developed an original script in the Kingdom of Bamum around 1895.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nBamum is a tonal Eastern Grassfields language with an original script, while religion is not documented.\n\n- **Bamum language** (*Bamum*) — Bamum is also called Shü Pamom, Shümom, Shupamem, Bamun, or Bamoun.\n- **Shü Pamom** — Shü Pamom is one of the names used for the Bamum language.\n- **Shümom** — Shümom is one of the names used for the Bamum language.\n- **Shupamem** — Shupamem is one of the names used for the Bamum language.\n- **Bamoun** — Bamoun is one of the names used for the Bamum language.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Bamum* — Name of the language\n- *Shü Pamom* — Name of the language\n- *Shümom* — Name of the language\n- *Shupamem* — Name of the language\n- *Bamun* — Name of the people and language\n- *Bamoun* — Name of the language\n- *Bamum script* — Original script developed around 1895\n- *King Njoya* — King linked to the script’s development\n- *Claude Ndam* — Cameroonian musician who sang in Bamum\n- *Bamum language* — An Eastern Grassfields language\n- *Eastern Grassfields* — Language group\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Emmanuel Matateyou, *Parlons Bamoun*, Paris: L’Harmattan, 2002.\n- Abdoulaye Laziz Nchare, *The Grammar of Shupamem*, PhD dissertation, New York University, 2012.\n- Solange Pawou Molu, *Problèmes de morphophonologie nominale en Bamun-Shüpamom*, PhD dissertation, Paris Cité University, 2018.\n- Bamum Scripts and Archives Project; Charles Riley, *Towards the Encoding of the Bamum Script in the UCS*, 2006.\n- Wikipedia, [“Bamun language”](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamun_language)\n- British Museum collection records for the arm-dagger, smoking-pipes, bag, masks, staff finial, figures, and related objects.\n- Museum of Ethnography catalogue records for Bamun objects.\n",
